\newcommand{\ResultsFigureDirectory}{../../reports/results/tfg_shared_modes/figures}
\newcommand{\PrimarySeeds}{24}
\newcommand{\AblationSeeds}{12}
\newcommand{\ScenarioCount}{27}
\newcommand{\AbstractResults}{The condition-balanced TFG change relative to shared QMEKF is -0.04\% (95\% CI -0.06 to -0.02\%) for heave and -0.28\% (95\% CI -0.40 to -0.16\%) for horizontal position. There are 0 flagged estimator trajectories across all registered operational and diagnostic cells. Relative to independent-axis TFG, the shared prior changes heave by -5.93\% (95\% CI -6.19 to -5.67\%) and horizontal position by -2.81\% (95\% CI -2.93 to -2.68\%); relative to independent QMEKF, shared TFG changes them by -5.95\% (95\% CI -6.20 to -5.69\%) and -3.09\% (95\% CI -3.23 to -2.95\%). Under reduced heave shared TFG has 4.3/20.9 mm heave/horizontal error against 5.4/23.4 mm independently, with 0/24 heading failures; the legacy excitation cap gives 25.7/112.0 mm and 7/12 heading failures in a paired 12-seed diagnosis.}
\newcommand{\RegressionResults}{The accompanying regression record lists the original and post-refactor gates and exact paired numerical differences. Existing thresholds are retained. The full-wrapper coupling-off smoke uses the existing TFG/CARMA 1\% agreement criterion; the process-law identities use $10^{-11}$ relative tolerance.}
\newcommand{\ResultsDiscussion}{Condition-balanced heave changes by -0.04\% (95\% CI -0.06 to -0.02\%) and horizontal position by -0.28\% (95\% CI -0.40 to -0.16\%) when replacing shared QMEKF with shared TFG. No scenario/endpoints meet both the predeclared 5\% improvement margin and the Holm significance criterion; 2 show statistically supported worsening, at most +0.48\%. The complete per-condition table prevents small aggregate changes from hiding different behavior in weak excitation, maneuvers or outages. The interaction table and figure evaluate nonadditivity directly. There are 0 flagged trajectories; their identities and the remaining consistency defects are retained below. Exact conditional-prior algebra does not make the inherited wave/bias/attitude approximation statistically calibrated. In the nominal case, joint physical NEES is 39.9 for shared TFG and 40.5 for shared QMEKF, against a 9-state reference mean of 9. This diagnostic is not repaired by the geometry change.}
\newcommand{\PerformanceResults}{On this host, the median of four counterbalanced repetitions is 4.28 us per 200 Hz core step for shared TFG, versus 2.68 us for shared QMEKF (ratio 1.60). With 25\% interval jitter the corresponding times are 5.73 and 4.03 us. Timings include 0.1 s model commits and magnetic updates at 25 Hz; percentiles and maxima are reported separately, and host scheduling can affect their tails.}
\newcommand{\FirmwareResults}{The Xtensa probe gives 14472 bytes for the shared TFG core versus 12504 for shared QMEKF. Their largest emitted function stack frames are 1744 and 1888 bytes; the static update-chain lower bounds are 3664 and 4864 bytes. The compiler used for this probe is Espressif GCC esp-14.2.0\_20241119 with \texttt{-Os}. The probe and the firmware builds predate the structured sample-path kernels and have not been repeated. A full Arduino firmware build is recorded separately in the reproducibility bundle. With matched Arduino optimization flags, the complete AtomS3R shared-mode application links at 1,029,687 bytes for TFG and 1,094,467 bytes for QMEKF; both use 31,660 bytes of globals. These figures exclude the estimator allocated at construction, whose core size is tabulated separately. The board package is ESP32 3.3.7 with M5Unified 0.2.13 and Espressif GCC 14.2.0\_20251107. The 200 Hz measurement harness also compiles, but its serial timing/heap/stack results require a connected board.}
\newcommand{\VerificationResults}{Measured maximum prediction finite-difference discrepancy is $3.23\times10^{-10}$; the relative independent continuous-covariance discrepancy is $6.27\times10^{-11}$. The anisotropic model-coordinate covariance discrepancy is $8.52\times10^{-11}$. Allocation-guarded float execution explicitly forces a falling-confidence fallback and remains finite with symmetric covariance; round-off eigenvalues are recorded rather than hidden.}
\newcommand{\FailureDiscussion}{Reduced heave is the condition that separates the cap policies. With the default policy, mean heave/horizontal errors are 4.3/21.0 mm for shared QMEKF and 4.3/20.9 mm for shared TFG, against 5.4/23.4 and 5.4/23.4 mm independently; 0 and 0 of 24 shared runs exceed 30 degrees RMS heading. In the paired 12-seed diagnosis (Table~\ref{tab:weak}) the legacy cap, which scales the cross term down, gives 21.8 and 25.7 mm heave, 89.9 and 56.4 degrees mean heading RMS and 12 and 7 heading failures. Cap targets 0.7 and 0.9 give 4.2 and 5.1 mm heave for shared TFG, against 4.4 mm at the default 0.8. Without confidence weighting the estimated coupling gives 3.7 and 3.7 mm and the known coupling 3.8 and 3.8 mm, so the estimator recovers the known-coupling benefit and confidence weighting gives up part of it in this condition. The real-only control maps only the co-spectrum and gives 7.4 and 7.0 mm, worse than the independent filters, so the quadrature term is required here. Omitting reset transport gives 4.4 mm shared-TFG heave, so the transport is immaterial in this condition.}
\newcommand{\HeaveSweepDiscussion}{A heave-RAO dose response (Tables~\ref{tab:heave-sweep} and~\ref{tab:heave-sf2}, Fig.~\ref{fig:heave-sweep}) repeats the first 12 primary seeds with the heave RAO scaled by $k$; horizontal motion and every sensor stream are unchanged within a seed. Its repeated $k=1$ and $k=0.15$ cells reproduce the heave, horizontal, heading, tilt and NEES scores of all 132 overlapping primary and diagnosis runs exactly. With the legacy cap the known prior's applied multiple coherence is 0.334 at $k=1$ and grows as $1/k^2$ (0.523, 0.685, 0.936 at $k=0.8, 0.7, 0.6$), reaching the 0.999 cap for $k\le0.5$. The legacy shared-QMEKF heave change relative to legacy real-only coupling is -5.5\% [-5.8, -5.1] at $k=1$ and -16.5\% [-17.6, -15.5] at $k=0.6$, but +62.4\% [+55.8, +68.9] at $k=0.5$, where the cap binds; the legacy heading interval first exceeds the independent filter by one degree at $k=0.4$ (QMEKF) and $k=0.3$ (TFG). Raising only the horizontal prior factor to 2 moves the predicted binding boundary to $k=0.29$ and the observed one to between 0.3 and 0.25. The capped legacy prior is nearly singular along the coupling direction: 99.9\% of the along-wave horizontal variance is tied to heave through an understated regression, while the cross-wave direction keeps its full variance. The default policy keeps the regression and holds the applied coherence at 0.8 by enlarging the horizontal prior along the coupling direction only. Across the 12 cells, shared TFG then has the lowest mean heave RMS of the four filters in 12 and the lowest horizontal RMS in 9 (at most 0.02\% above the lowest elsewhere); relative to independent TFG its heave changes by -27.9\% to -5.7\% and its horizontal error by -9.8\% to -1.4\% over the default-spreading factors, and 0 shared runs exceed 30 degrees heading.}
\newcommand{\SupplementDiscussion}{The pinned eight-record replay gives heave changes between -0.0127\% and -0.0007\%, too small to justify a geometry switch. These intervals condition on each wave record and resample only sensors/initialization. The 24-seed full-rate transition supplement finds no practically meaningful recovery advantage; 0 transition recoveries are censored. Its exported positions have 10-micrometre resolution, so sub-resolution differences are not evidence for useful improvement. The six-condition reset supplement shows small ordinary-case position changes for both geometries. Reversed unchecked coupling degrades horizontal error by 7.55\% in rapid rotations, while confidence reduces that diagnostic to the uncoupled response. Confidence is useful but does not guarantee robustness.}
\newcommand{\SecondaryDiscussion}{Secondary attitude and bias results are condition dependent. At 800 rpm, shared TFG reduces mean tilt RMS from 0.45 to 0.31 degrees, reduces heading from 3.02 to 2.10 degrees and reduces accelerometer-bias error from 7.84 to 5.40 mg. Under colored noise it reduces heading from 3.73 to 2.47 degrees, while horizontal position changes by +0.48\% [+0.34, +0.62]. During maneuvers it increases heading from 1.75 to 2.28 degrees: paired absolute change +0.53 degrees, marginal 95\% CI [+0.37, +0.68]; gyro-bias RMS changes by +10.02\% [+6.86, +13.18]. These secondary intervals are exploratory and are not part of the Holm-controlled primary position family.}
\newcommand{\Recommendation}{Shared TFG is the most accurate of the four estimators in the condition-balanced position comparison: it improves on shared QMEKF, independent TFG and independent QMEKF with paired intervals excluding zero for heave and horizontal position. Most of the gain comes from the shared prior (-5.93\% (95\% CI -6.19 to -5.67\%) heave, -2.81\% (95\% CI -2.93 to -2.68\%) horizontal); the geometry adds -0.04\% (95\% CI -0.06 to -0.02\%) and -0.28\% (95\% CI -0.40 to -0.16\%) at 1.6 times the host cost of shared QMEKF. The shared prior is worth keeping in both geometries with the cap policy; TFG is the better choice only where its small accuracy and attitude gains justify the added computation. The largest condition-specific cost against independent QMEKF is +0.41\% horizontal RMS (Colored sensor noise). Nominal joint NEES remains poorly calibrated. Actual ESP32 timing and stack high-water measurements are still required. The shipping OU-III default remains unchanged.}
